<article class="env-remark" id="07KH">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/07KH">Remark <span data-tag="07KH">60.5.4</span>.</a> In Situation <a href="/tag/07MD" data-tag="07MD">60.5.1</a> we denote $\text{Cris}^\wedge (C/A)$ the category whose objects are pairs $(B \to C, \delta )$ such that </p>
  <p><ol>
  <li><p>
$B$ is a $p$-adically complete $A$-algebra, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$B \to C$ is a surjection of $A$-algebras, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$\delta $ is a divided power structure on $\mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(B \to C)$, 
</p>
</li><li><p>
$A \to B$ is a homomorphism of divided power rings. 
</p>
</li>
</ol></p>
  <p> Morphisms are defined as in Definition <a href="/tag/07HM" data-tag="07HM">60.5.2</a>. Then $\text{Cris}(C/A) \subset \text{Cris}^\wedge (C/A)$ is the full subcategory consisting of those $B$ such that $p$ is nilpotent in $B$. Conversely, any object $(B \to C, \delta )$ of $\text{Cris}^\wedge (C/A)$ is equal to the limit </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  (B \to C, \delta ) = \mathop{\mathrm{lim}}\nolimits _ e (B/p^ eB \to C, \delta )  \]
</div>
  <p> where for $e \gg 0$ the object $(B/p^ eB \to C, \delta )$ lies in $\text{Cris}(C/A)$, see Divided Power Algebra, Lemma <a href="/tag/07KD" data-tag="07KD">23.4.5</a>. In particular, we see that $\text{Cris}^\wedge (C/A)$ is a full subcategory of the category of pro-objects of $\text{Cris}(C/A)$, see Categories, Remark <a href="/tag/05PX" data-tag="05PX">4.22.5</a>. </p>
</article>